      "content": "RESOURCES FOR TRANSFER STUDENTS",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "69da6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 3,
      "content": "Cal State Apply Cal State LA College of Natural and Social Sciences College of Natural and Social Sciences Academic Advisement Center Degree with a Guarantee Department of Anthropology General Education Lower Division Courses University Catalog",
      "contentHtml": "<p><a href=\"https://www.calstate.edu/apply\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Cal State Apply</a></p><p><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Cal State LA</a></p><p><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/nss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">College of Natural and Social Sciences</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.calstatela.edu/nss/advising\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">College of Natural and Social Sciences Academic Advisement Center</a></p><p><a href=\"http://adegreewithaguarantee.com/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Degree with a Guarantee</a></p><p><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/academic/anthro\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Department of Anthropology</a></p><p><a href=\"https://ecatalog.calstatela.edu/content.php?catoid=77&amp;navoid=11897\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">General Education Lower Division Courses</a></p><p><a href=\"http://ecatalog.calstatela.edu/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">University Catalog</a></p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "6ada6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralTitle",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 4,
      "content": "DIVERSITY REQUIREMENT FOR CAL STATE LA GRADUATION",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "afdd7145-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 5,
      "content": "The articulation information in Assist.org not only shows a link to the admission requirements (aka our Major Specific Criteria webpage) but also Cal State LA graduation requirements that may be completed at the lower-division level, prior to transferring to the University. The diversity requirement is a graduation requirement , not an admission requirement. The diversity requirement may be completed prior to, or after, transferring to the University. For the Cal State LA degree, students will be required to complete two courses (six units) certified as diversity courses, at least one focusing on issues of race and ethnicity and their intersectionality with other social categories that structure inequality in society. These courses can be completed either at the lower-division or upper-division level from among courses satisfying GE requirements. Please view the General Education agreements on Assist.org for the lower-division courses that will meet the Diversity (d) and Race &amp; Ethnicity (re) Requirements.",
      "contentHtml": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The articulation information in Assist.org not only shows a link to the admission requirements (aka our </span><a href=\"http://www.calstatela.edu/admissions/major-specific-criteria\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Major Specific Criteria </a><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">webpage) but also Cal State LA graduation requirements that may be completed at the lower-division level, prior to transferring to the University. The diversity requirement is a </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(169, 62, 58);\">graduation requirement</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">, not an admission requirement. The diversity requirement may be completed prior to, or after, transferring to the University. </span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">For the Cal State LA degree, students will be required to </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(169, 62, 58);\">complete two courses (six units)</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> certified as diversity courses, at least one focusing on issues of race and ethnicity and their intersectionality with other social categories that structure inequality in society. These courses can be completed either at the lower-division or upper-division level from among courses satisfying GE requirements.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Please view the </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(169, 62, 58);\">General Education </strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">agreements on Assist.org for the lower-division courses that will meet the </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Diversity (d)</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> and </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Race &amp; Ethnicity (re)</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> Requirements.</span></p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "67da6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralTitle",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 6,
      "content": "ANTHROPOLOGY, B.A.",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "68da6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 7,
      "content": "Bachelor of Arts Degree The Bachelor of Arts degree in Anthropology is available for those who seek a liberal arts education or who wish to prepare for advanced study and research. The total number of units required for the Bachelor of Arts degree in Anthropology is 120 units, of which 52-54 units are in the major. Consult with an advisor for the specific number of units required in all areas of the degree including GE and free electives. Departmental Honors Program The Department of Anthropology offers an honors program for students who have completed at least 12 upper division units in anthropology, who have a minimum 3.25 grade point average in all anthropology courses, and who possess a minimum 3.0 grade point average in all college work. Application to the program is made by written petition. The program is designed to introduce superior students to intensive research experience in anthropology and related fields, as well as to more sophisticated and thorough study of anthropological theory in relevant subareas of the discipline. Honor students are permitted to depart from the normal major program in various ways to broaden and enrich their curriculum. Honor students plan their programs in consultation with an honors adviser. Students enroll during each of their final semesters in ANTH 3960 , and submit a written thesis to the departmental honors committee. Diplomas and transcripts of honors program graduates are designated: \"Graduated with Departmental Honors in Anthropology.\"",
      "contentHtml": "<p><strong style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Bachelor of Arts Degree</strong></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The Bachelor of Arts degree in Anthropology is available for those who seek a liberal arts education or who wish to prepare for advanced study and research.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The total number of units required for the Bachelor of Arts degree in Anthropology is 120 units, of which 52-54 units are in the major. Consult with an advisor for the specific number of units required in all areas of the degree including GE and free electives.</span></p><p><br></p><p><strong style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Departmental Honors Program</strong></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The Department of Anthropology offers an honors program for students who have completed at least 12 upper division units in anthropology, who have a minimum 3.25 grade point average in all anthropology courses, and who possess a minimum 3.0 grade point average in all college work. Application to the program is made by written petition.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The program is designed to introduce superior students to intensive research experience in anthropology and related fields, as well as to more sophisticated and thorough study of anthropological theory in relevant subareas of the discipline. Honor students are permitted to depart from the normal major program in various ways to broaden and enrich their curriculum.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Honor students plan their programs in consultation with an honors adviser. Students enroll during each of their final semesters in </span><strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: rgb(43, 134, 24);\">ANTH 3960</strong><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">, and submit a written thesis to the departmental honors committee. Diplomas and transcripts of honors program graduates are designated:</span><em style=\"font-size: 16px;\"> \"Graduated with Departmental Honors in Anthropology.\"</em></p><p><br></p>"
    }
